\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ 0 & \\alpha c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalso belongs to $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $T$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-107119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $E$ containing all diagonal $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $E,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $E,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a diagonal matrix. This matrix belongs to $E.$ So, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)=f(-1)}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =f(-1)+g(-1) \\\\  & =(f+g)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(1) & =kf(1) \\\\  & =kf(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-43779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Q$ is closed under function addition\n- $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $Q$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "IV only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $W$ is closed under function addition\n- $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-2,2]\\to R$ and its subset $E$ containing all even functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $E$ is closed under function addition\n- $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $E$ is a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n- $E$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-2,2]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in E,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(-x) & =f(-x)+g(-x) \\\\  & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =(f+g)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in E$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(-x) & =kf(-x) \\\\  & =kf(x) \\\\  & =(kf)(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $E$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $E$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-94642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under function addition\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f3c22dad9706a70e79d98d7855394f805d1595baed1e24c949dc02ab67d581d8"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II  only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "q-107139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/state.json" "file_sha256" "bdf564146f6135e645bb3e614fbcd2d8a374e30df68b0b88e6a706986d6aec7e"} {"question" "e-8234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-8234.json" "file_sha256" "5c9b037f16a3791cc6665b21693f73091ecdd362a5e504849afe88b696f57697"} {"question" "e-8234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}-{b}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}-{b}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{3}-bt) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{3}-(kb)t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-8234.json" "file_sha256" "5c9b037f16a3791cc6665b21693f73091ecdd362a5e504849afe88b696f57697"} {"question" "e-8233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-8233.json" "file_sha256" "d2a41879f726b84946050a02f1f32ebbe1a62cae2727ddf4d4f67f2de31b93b5"} {"question" "e-8233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0.$ Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$ This matrix belongs to $N,$  and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0.$ If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0.$ This matrix does not belong to $N,$  and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-8233.json" "file_sha256" "d2a41879f726b84946050a02f1f32ebbe1a62cae2727ddf4d4f67f2de31b93b5"} {"question" "e-4813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: | \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-4813.json" "file_sha256" "a2cca421b60cdee30f45c1fde0a2ccb787e125297bcb1014e37474f4ba345852"} {"question" "e-4813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =-f(2)-g(2) \\\\  & =-(f(2)+g(2)) \\\\  & =-(f+g)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $f+g \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-f(2)) \\\\  & =-kf(2) \\\\  & =-(kf)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $kf \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064503/example-4813.json" "file_sha256" "a2cca421b60cdee30f45c1fde0a2ccb787e125297bcb1014e37474f4ba345852"}] "usage" {"e-4813" [] "e-8234" [] "q-43769" [] "e-8233" []} "basis" 2155 "retractions" [[17592186125069 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $R_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$, and its subset $B = {t^{2} + at + b \\mid a, b ∈ R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $B$ is a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$\n4. $B$ is a **not** a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$"] [17592186124320 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3$, and its subset $A = \\big\\{at^3-bt \\: \\mid \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$"] [17592186124320 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A$, then\n$f(t) + g(t)|= (a_{1}t^{3} - b_{1}t) + (a_{2}t^{3} - b_{2}t); = (a_{1} + a_{2})t^{3} - (b_{1} + b_{2})t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$kf(t)|= k(at^{3} - bt); = (ka)t^{3} - (kb)t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""] [17592186124319 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$, and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n4. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$"] [17592186124319 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0$. Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$. This matrix belongs to $N$, and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0$. If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0$. This matrix does not belong to $N$, and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""] [17592186124216 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: \\mid \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under function addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$"] [17592186124216 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A$, then\n$(f + g)(0)|= f(0) + g(0); =-f(2) - g(2); = - (f(2) + g(2)); = - (f + g)(2)$,\nwhich means $f+g \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$(kf)(0)|= kf(0); = k \\cdot (-f(2)); =-kf(2); = - (kf)(2)$,\nwhich means $kf \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123437 17592186123438 17592186123439 17592186123440 17592186123441] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123436 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123436 :answer-field/id] [17592186123436 :answer-field/key] [17592186123436 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
